will-server for Debian
----------------------

The server speaks only with TLS, on port 17771: it does not start until its
certificate and key lie in /etc/will-server/tls (see TLS below).

After installation the systemd unit `will-server.service` is enabled.
Logs: journalctl -u will-server -f

The daemon runs as the unprivileged system user `will-server`.
The databases are stored in /var/lib/will-server: will.eternity.db, will.space.db
and will.time.db (--db-path /var/lib/will-server, set by will-server-start).
The directory must exist: the server does not create it.

Device-token authentication
---------------------------

Clients authenticate with a persistent device token sent via BindToken on connect.
Each unique token maps to one body (one user per device). The first token on an
empty server is born a man at once; every later new token is an unborn body that
waits until someone who may let in at the gates (Врата) bears it there.

TLS
---

The server speaks with TLS on port 17771, and only so, once a certificate and its
key lie in /etc/will-server/tls; until then it does not start. Make them on this
server, that the key never leaves it:

  cd /etc/will-server/tls
  sudo openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes \
      -days 3650 -subj "/CN=will" -addext "subjectAltName=IP:<server address>" \
      -keyout server.key -out server.crt
  sudo chown root:will-server server.key server.crt
  sudo chmod 0640 server.key
  sudo systemctl restart will-server

The certificate (server.crt) is public: it is built into the clients, which trust
it alone. Open port 17771 in the firewall. Flags: --tls-cert, --tls-key, --tls-port;
the service starts the server with --no-plain, so nothing listens in the open.

Scaling (single process)
------------------------

will-server uses gRPC (HTTP/2) with a synchronous Session handler per client,
suitable for roughly 2k concurrent sessions on one host, depending on CPU, RAM,
and message rate.

Recommended production settings:

- systemd LimitNOFILE=65535 (already set in will-server.service)
- Ensure the process ulimits allow open files: ulimit -n
- Optional CLI flags:
    --max-clients 4096
    --keepalive-interval 30
    --keepalive-timeout 10

Load testing:

  will-server --port 7770 &
  will-load-clients --host 127.0.0.1 --port 7770 --clients 2000 --hold-seconds 10

Acceptance: 2000 idle sessions with failures=0 in load_clients output.
